# Example 07:

# Let's dig deeper into the cyclophosphamide result. To begin with, let's find the proteins that connect the drug to
# neoplasms, using the two functions that potential_disease_treatments is built on:

match
let $dr, $p in drug_protein_associations();
let $di, $p in disease_protein_associations();
$di has name "Neoplasms";
$dr has dgidb-name "CYCLOPHOSPHAMIDE";
$p has uniprot-id $id;
fetch { "protein": $id };

# We get a single protein, P11245. Neoplasms and cyclophosphamide each have a direct interaction with this protein, so
# the potential treatment is a case of the drug affecting a protein that impacts the spread of the cancer. We can
# confirm this with the lower-level interaction functions:

match
let $dr, $p in drug_protein_interactions();
let $di, $p in disease_protein_interactions();
$di has name "Neoplasms";
$dr has dgidb-name "CYCLOPHOSPHAMIDE";
$p has uniprot-id $id;
fetch { "protein": $id };

# We can dig even deeper to uncover the data behind those interactions, but this depth is sufficient to understand how
# the treatment might work. Obviously, this treatment may not work, but this information serves as a good indicator to
# begin the first stages of a clinical trial, and is a key step of the drug discovery process. By using functions to
# identify potential treatments in this way, selection of candidate drugs for trials can be vastly accelerated.

